):$\n\n- Multiples of $10:$ $\\,10,\\,20,\\,30,\\,40,\\,50,\\,60,\\,70,…$\n\n- Multiples of $12:$ $\\,12,\\,24,\\,36,\\,48,\\,60,\\,72,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(10,12)=60.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(y+4)^{5}$ and $(y+4)^{3},$ we raise $(y+4)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((y+4)^{5},\\,(y+4)^{3})=(y+4)^{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(10(y+4)^{5},\\,12(y+4)^{3})=60\\cdot (y+4)^{5}=60(y+4)^{5}.\n$$" 13194139535574 17592186045443 true] [17592186518583 1080 "12(w-2)^{7}" 13194139535574 17592186045443 true] [17592186518583 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535574 17592186045443 true] [17592186518583 1083 #uuid "038e34d7-2dec-5210-ac64-7d3db5817c9b" 13194139535574 17592186045443 true] [17592186518584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518608 13194139535574 17592186045443 true] [17592186518584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535574 17592186045443 true] [17592186518584 1071 "The least common multiple of $9(x+8)^{3}$ and $5(x+8)$ is {{field-1}}." 13194139535574 17592186045443 true] [17592186518584 1072 #uuid "07b1d066-f62b-5882-a508-7ac492a0feff" 13194139535574 17592186045443 true] [17592186518584 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(9,5):$\n\n- Multiples of $9:$ $\\,9,\\,18,\\,27,\\,36,\\,45,\\,54,…$\n\n- Multiples of $5:$ $\\,5,\\,10,\\,15,\\,20,\\,25,\\,30,\\,35,\\,40,\\,45,\\,50,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(9,5)=45.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(x+8)^{3}$ and $(x+8),$ we raise $(x+8)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((x+8)^{3},\\,(x+8))=(x+8)^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(9(x+8)^{3},\